13:10:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You usually can’t be *completely sure* of any public figure’s integrity, but you can build a well-supported judgment from evidence rather than reputation or charisma. Look for patterns over time: - **Check specific claims.** Compare what they say with primary sources, official records, reputable local reporting, and independent fact-checkers. Focus on claims that can actually be verified. - **Compare promises to actions.** Did they vote, govern, fundraise, or negotiate in ways consistent with their stated principles—especially when it was politically inconvenient? - **Watch how they handle errors.** Honest people can be wrong. A better sign is whether they acknowledge mistakes, correct the record, and explain what changed rather than denying obvious facts. - **Examine conflicts of interest.** Review required financial disclosures, donor relationships, business ties, lobbying connections, and whether they support transparency rules that apply to themselves. - **Seek independent criticism and praise.** Read reporting from outlets and watchdogs with different political perspectives. If only allies consider them honest, that is weaker evidence than if credible critics also concede their fairness or accuracy. - **Distinguish vagueness from candor.** Integrity often shows up in clear, testable positions and honest discussion of tradeoffs—not just appealing slogans. - **Consider their treatment of opponents and institutions.** Do they misrepresent rivals, encourage harassment, evade oversight, or attack every unfavorable source as corrupt? Those can be warning signs. - **Update your view when new evidence appears.** Avoid assuming either that all politicians are dishonest or that a favored politician is uniquely trustworthy. A useful standard is: *Would you still regard their behavior as principled if someone from the other political side did the same thing?* Applying the same criteria consistently is one of the best ways to assess integrity fairly.
